Power management tools for laptops and desktops on Artix Linux
(OpenRC-based, no systemd).

@code bash
# ACPI battery and AC adapter status
acpi                             # battery status summary
acpi -b                          # battery info
acpi -a                          # AC adapter status
acpi -t                          # thermal zone temperatures
acpi -V                          # all ACPI information
acpi -i                          # battery design capacity and health

@text
UPower provides a D-Bus interface for power device enumeration.

@code bash
# Install upower
sudo pacman -S upower

# List power devices
upower -e                        # enumerate devices
upower -i /org/freedesktop/UPower/devices/battery_BAT0
upower -d                        # dump all device info

# Monitor power events
upower --monitor                 # watch for changes in real time

@text
TLP is an advanced power management tool that applies optimal settings
automatically. On Artix, use the OpenRC service.

@code bash
# Install TLP
sudo pacman -S tlp
sudo rc-update add tlp default   # enable at boot (OpenRC)
sudo rc-service tlp start        # start now

# Check current status
sudo tlp-stat                    # full status report
sudo tlp-stat -s                 # system info
sudo tlp-stat -b                 # battery info
sudo tlp-stat -d                 # disk info
sudo tlp-stat -p                 # PCI device power state
sudo tlp-stat -u                 # USB device info
sudo tlp-stat -t                 # temperatures

# Manual mode switching
sudo tlp bat                     # force battery mode
sudo tlp ac                      # force AC mode
sudo tlp start                   # apply auto settings

# Configuration: /etc/tlp.conf
# Key settings:
#   CPU_SCALING_GOVERNOR_ON_AC=performance
#   CPU_SCALING_GOVERNOR_ON_BAT=powersave
#   RUNTIME_PM_ON_BAT=auto
#   USB_AUTOSUSPEND=1

@text
powertop shows power consumption by process and device. Use it to
identify power-hungry processes and tune settings.

@code bash
# Install powertop
sudo pacman -S powertop

# Interactive power monitor (requires root)
sudo powertop

# Tabs: Overview, Idle Stats, Frequency Stats, Device Stats, Tunables
# In Tunables tab, press Enter to toggle Good/Bad

# Generate HTML report
sudo powertop --html=power-report.html

# Auto-tune all settings (one-shot)
sudo powertop --auto-tune

# Calibrate (run on battery, takes several minutes)
sudo powertop --calibrate

@text
Suspend and hibernate on Artix with OpenRC. These use kernel interfaces
directly or through elogind.

@code bash
# Suspend to RAM (sleep)
loginctl suspend                 # via elogind
echo mem | sudo tee /sys/power/state   # direct kernel interface

# Hibernate (suspend to disk, requires swap >= RAM)
loginctl hibernate               # via elogind
echo disk | sudo tee /sys/power/state  # direct kernel interface

# Hybrid sleep (suspend + hibernate image)
loginctl hybrid-sleep

# Check available sleep states
cat /sys/power/state             # mem disk standby freeze
cat /sys/power/mem_sleep         # s2idle [deep]  (deep = S3)

# Set deep sleep (S3) instead of s2idle
echo deep | sudo tee /sys/power/mem_sleep

# elogind handles lid close, power button, etc.
# Config: /etc/elogind/logind.conf
#   HandleLidSwitch=suspend
#   HandlePowerKey=poweroff
#   IdleAction=suspend
#   IdleActionSec=30min
